// Phases of One Night — the transfer policy of one piece.
//
// It is `pk(owner)`. Compile this file and `../nc/transfer.roost` and compare the reports: same
// policy, same 34 bytes, same Poseidon377 root. **A unique piece of art and a unit of money
// carry the identical policy**, and that is the most useful thing in this repository to have
// understood.
//
// Everything that makes this an NFT rather than money is in `asset_id`, not here:
//
//     terms    = Fq(visibility + 2*max_supply + 2^65*index)
//     asset_id = Poseidon_3(DS_ASSET, collection_id, label, terms)
//
// (`spec/note-format-v0.md` section 8.) `max_supply = 1` and `decimals = 0` make the asset
// indivisible and unique; `index` places it in the collection; `collection_id` — derived from
// the issuer's key, a collection label and the channel id — is what groups the hundred pieces.
// Changing any of them yields a different `asset_id`. So "there is only one of these" is
// **checked by every verifier**, not claimed in `c.json` and not enforced by a clause.
//
// Which is why there is no clause here that says "this cannot be split". It would be dead code:
// the conservation rule in the circuit cannot produce two units of an asset whose cap is one,
// whatever the policy says.

note Piece(owner: Key) {
  spend { by owner }
}

check "the holder gives the piece away" {
  spend Piece()
  amount 1
  as owner
  expect spend
}

check "nobody else can" {
  spend Piece()
  amount 1
  as nobody
  expect cannot
}

// Not a separate rule and not enforced here: the amount is 1 because the asset's cap is 1. This
// check spends the whole note, which is the only thing a note of amount 1 can do.
check "the whole piece, or nothing" {
  spend Piece()
  amount 1
  as owner
  expect spend
}
